{"title":"2005 IMO Shortlist Problems/A2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Resources\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Resources</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(<i>Bulgaria</i>)\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba48837cbe425e0a603921abb6d4be1caf212c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^+$\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> denote the set of all postive real numbers.  Determine all functions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/f/6bfbfc5adab84d34a18d13ed30a4556116059e90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f: \\mathbb{R}^+ \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^+$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"100\" height=\"18\" /> such that\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/3/73332fe027c228a6b3a7e5a66e53bb96f177110b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x)f(y) = 2f(x+yf(x))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"204\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>for all positive real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4e6ba8781d42ee434379ac570532e425e7b55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"9\" height=\"11\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><b>Lemma 1.</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is non-decreasing.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i>  Suppose, on the contrary, that there exist <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/4/654a47e55a6ead91198efcef584d157001831113.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt; z$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/2/372dc9221a513c8d4ce379bbee7d42c686bd99dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) &lt; f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" />.  We set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25efcbf4653ae1533520c2d4214a9b39d3121cdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = \\frac{x-z}{f(z)-f(x)} &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"162\" height=\"37\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/8/ea8481445fafaa59bc3e450051973655ca1cb04f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + yf(x) = z + yf(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"175\" height=\"18\" />, to obtain\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/e/00ea4c3d3800cf8efed2ef825c8f5ba2f9fb207a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)f(y) = 2f(x+yf(x)) = 2f(z+yf(z)) = f(z)f(y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"431\" height=\"18\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/7/0e79eb952741dfaf648bcfc656c4a7fb512b239f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" />,  a contradiction.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2.</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is not strictly increasing.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i> Assume the contrary.  Then for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad2b13010966b055a23d4a8cdb44201048c3a51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x ,y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> we have\n</p>\n<center> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/9/6599d4b24cc6bae9ca7f1e6e4e3f282fe234bc9e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)f(y) = 2f(x + yf(x)) &gt; 2f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"273\" height=\"18\" />, </center>\n<p>so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/5/b753c751fa4943c0199dc5681554628d1099d628.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(y) &gt; 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />.  Furthermore, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Injective\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Injective\">injective</a>, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/8/6d8c20bc84bb48049db3cd3779cfc88bf70d0afd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x+1 \\cdot f(x)) = f(x)f(1) = f(1+ x\\cdot f(1))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"345\" height=\"18\" />, which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/2/532b78b535345d7914521ffb703b834df61aba91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+ f(x) = 1+ x \\cdot f(1))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"186\" height=\"18\" />, or\n</p>\n<center> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/3/2134bebbca8cee5568a7781f38536dfeb8423b4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = (f(1)-1)x + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" />. </center>\n<p>But then for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> arbitrarily close to 0, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c96dd6ec1dc4ad7520fbdc78fcdbec9edd068d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"20\" /> becomes less than 2, a contradiction.  Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is not strictly increasing.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p>Now, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/4/834e36e7eb6f0864e57bef50912bd599f6853636.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;z$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/7/0e79eb952741dfaf648bcfc656c4a7fb512b239f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" />.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is in the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/e/24e339285088b9d6a9f9cdbe684f7765abd7f80e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, (x-z)/f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"123\" height=\"18\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/a/c1a6c51ab3c1cb975bcf33a9811bed8626f8e2eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z&lt; z+yf(z) \\le x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" />, so\n</p>\n<center>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/8/e389c842700fcbbbdbda1e345b9b9c932008c124.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z) \\le f(z+yf(z)) \\le f(x) = f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"275\" height=\"18\" />, </center>\n<p>so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b157baaf8dcc4bf26668f7ab519ac2e1e9089d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x+yf(z)) = f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"159\" height=\"18\" />.  It follows that\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/6/7d652d3c80adf1bc027858063284afdc302801fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)f(y) = 2f(z + yf(z)) = 2f(x) = 2f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"337\" height=\"18\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/b/ddbcc246cc055dffacd8be53f9c72a5543ad2a7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(y) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />, for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in that interval.\n</p><p>But if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/4/56400960efbf3660c8e10726f1dbdcf3bea48690.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${} f(y_0) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />, then setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/8/df85509b8ce5072326c400ae113be212aa0e9701.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=y=y_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"83\" height=\"11\" /> in the original equation gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/1/4417bb35cd1290de2cc63d3734906d2e3ca8cbfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(3^n y_0) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" />, by induction.  It follows that for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, there exist <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/8/0a8683a227df3f86e4ea356d78642966edf6f7a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;x$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"10\" />  and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/8/078bbb5820160a6bb987f9a3adc8ba616d60b51d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c&gt;x$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/f/5cf4bf97a83967bbd7ec93bb1036301af6a491e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(a) = f(c) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"124\" height=\"18\" />, and since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is non-decreasing, we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/2/ff2c2adb87dba4e441f204f7ffabf89daa4ee077.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />.  It is easy to see that this satisfies the given equation.  Q.E.D.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><b>Lemma 1.</b> There exist distinct positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/8/2c897d0558f286c637da0d0707564a06cf48b6b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a,b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/e/77e0d5699ecab14b4db1c3f4ff568138448e9e1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(a) = f(b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i>  Suppose the contrary, i.e., suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/8/228663427166fa0dd8911ea5e0d4c3ddfd5ded79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" /> is <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Injective\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Injective\">injective</a>.  Then for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, we have\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/5/4e51d3532e5d3e7a7f7f46afdd506227322147c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x+1\\cdot f(x)) = f(x)f(1) = f(1 + x\\cdot f(1))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"345\" height=\"18\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>which implies\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/d/6fdade12f12a0fc5226c08bc59a992fc42f38d11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x + f(x) = 1 + f(1) \\cdot x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"181\" height=\"18\" /><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/7/6171dce746f7f913dc00134d135d4353f1c4aa5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) = (f(1)-1)x +1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>This means that either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/7/2b77f12bdf06a4fd4cecba1315a0ac5679c98b06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(1)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/e/7deb37c9b8566c4fc360160d3c27b42efc76bdf2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> (a contradiction, since that is not injective), or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/d/dfd373ab6bd65ea2f51f37d9fe27a4b072d0bb2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) = ax+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"110\" height=\"18\" />, for some real <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/6/526bd77b4de5c74c1a40d9bb93c66189acdebf36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a$\" width=\"9\" height=\"8\" />.  But setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ade6d8f702c509f43e524f6d4e30c085f6a1695.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y=x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"44\" height=\"11\" /> then gives us a quadratic in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> with a nonzero leading coefficient, which has at most two real roots, implying that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> can only assume two different values, a contradiction.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2.</b>  There exist <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/9/4392370afdede425fcaee843d46d822aa9192e52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle c_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> and infinitely many <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/6/796261b7fbada42116dab8547782a637fe1ceee6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle c_n = 3^n \\cdot c_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"86\" height=\"15\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/5/3d52c4c453c838b6f0ea07778725efbd8b211ce0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(c_n) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />, for all nonnegative integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i>  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/8/2c897d0558f286c637da0d0707564a06cf48b6b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a,b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" /> be the distinct positive reals of Lemma 1 such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/4/c9427079f236b58ee38e54370ce5380f0df7345d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(a)=f(b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />; without loss of generality, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/d/f1d81262f05b73d65ddd3aa76a136fba5eacad68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a&gt;b$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />.  Letting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/9/4899f0de22f337e3f92fc9b2bcb267a283eaf798.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle c_0 = \\frac{a-b}{f(b)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"81\" height=\"42\" /> yields\n</p>\n<center>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/d/ccd59a4ba0dd037ba7a18f0d2a2f6908531a1c68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(b)f(c_0) = 2f\\left( b + \\frac{a-b}{f(b)} \\right) = 2f(a) = 2f(b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"353\" height=\"43\" />. </center>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/2/c02f8c305c4b765c56b36f451c66874f166abf5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(b) \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"19\" />, this implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/a/23aaf6b7f426255e86ca3da3ea0a43b783fb60a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(c_0) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" />.  We now prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/5/3d52c4c453c838b6f0ea07778725efbd8b211ce0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(c_n) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> for all nonnegative <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, by induction.  We have just proven our base case.  Now, assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/5/3d52c4c453c838b6f0ea07778725efbd8b211ce0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(c_n) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />.  Setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/1/081aa739bbf2da4439fab23aebced46b387180f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x=y=c_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"84\" height=\"11\" /> gives us\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/4/c94e7d00b892a86a4b6bb2eed563a135e1fa599c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot 2 = f(c_n)f(c_n) = 2 f(c_n + c_n f(c_n)) = 2f(3c_n) = 2f(c_{n+1})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"470\" height=\"18\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/5/3c5e1aa0ec54511f9fc79c882ea99d5ce8456e2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(c_{n+1}) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"18\" />, as desired.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p><b>Lemma 3.</b> For all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/0/f00aa378f0404d597dbe5d4eb138d56d5be5bd6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\in \\mathbb{R}^+$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/4/18429d1e26940796845c17e7f59a05ef110bfde0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) \\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i> We note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/3/0c3b90253c07fb8c4c1afd43a9881c89e489b171.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y = \\frac{-x}{f(x)-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"103\" height=\"37\" /> is the solution to the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c77b225af39e75e49c4c7b100b26f6e0bc94afc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y  = x + y\\cdot f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"123\" height=\"18\" />, which is positive when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/2/34256a8bff5a23662afe9c098bb74d07ab00f980.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x)&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />, so if this is the case, setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4e6ba8781d42ee434379ac570532e425e7b55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"9\" height=\"11\" /> to this value gives us\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/6/9b668e827523004766fc22b77bf4eeeaa3d8db18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x)f(y) = 2f(y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"18\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>and since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/7/c071d3b41cb66e9d99265957e30214a4537f9507.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(y)&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />, this implies, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/c/fec96ab6e610d9c0c556b11d289a8bdf349eee20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) = 2 \\not&lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" />, a contradiction.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p><b>Lemma 4.</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/4/9b49341e8eef906f617e5f6d1ba602e900bed714.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) \\ge 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i>  We will prove by induction that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/8/92871c8390fad8325fffd23bd60d83abd6730a4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) \\ge 2^{1- \\frac{1}{2^n}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"22\" />, for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.  Our base case comes from Lemma 3.  Now, if for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/4/1a40ff086770fec7217c61252efe72d48438ee56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) \\ge 2^{1-\\frac{1}{2^n}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"22\" />, then for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/f/92f0f14d95ee05d626edff5beefd55475db83278.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle z$\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\" />,\n</p>\n<center>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/1/961756ada48786d9880fae1512167c09f2d52a3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[f(x)]^2 = f(x)f(x) = 2f(z) \\ge 2 \\cdot 2^{1-\\frac{1}{2^n}} = 2^{2\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2^{n+1}} \\right) }$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"408\" height=\"25\" />, </center>\n<p>so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/3/dc3be2dd65629f915d6702c7fca80b6fdea36aa9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) \\ge 2^{\\frac{1}{2^{n+1}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"23\" />, as desired.\n</p><p>Now, suppose there exists some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/e/d1e605105e5310a01b6914d438d08622ba76243b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) &lt; 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" />.  Then there exists some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/4/a044fb2d391d2d20f9395e64385fd4d82aef1ff3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{1-\\frac{1}{2^n}} &gt; f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"106\" height=\"24\" />.  But this gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/e/dbe89efde2d5eb33894df0d39e1c495a691183e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) \\ge 2^{1-\\frac{1}{2^n}} &gt; f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"162\" height=\"22\" />, a contradiction.  Thus for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/4/9b49341e8eef906f617e5f6d1ba602e900bed714.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) \\ge 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" />.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p>We will now prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/b/32bfa805386d3cb7e245ec57d8dec29ff15cae18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> is the only solution to the functional equation.  Consider any value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.  By Lemma 2, there exists some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/2/1c2e7b817478bbe57180658860b404f3cdc4dcdd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle {} c_n &gt; x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"12\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/5/3d52c4c453c838b6f0ea07778725efbd8b211ce0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(c_n) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />.  Setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/8/8f8ad949a202612d0e052a3b2becedbf926d61ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y= \\frac{c_n -x}{f(x)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"86\" height=\"37\" /> then gives us\n</p>\n<center> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/5/3e5a1a05ae2f47709e64797e34dbfc75b4240549.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) f\\left( \\frac{c_n -x}{f(x)} \\right) = 2f(c_n) = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"237\" height=\"43\" />. </center>\n<p>But from Lemma 4, we know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/c/09c5e0e8de31bb43c7f964eedddb202b50c6a072.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x), f\\left( \\frac{c_n-x}{f(x)} \\right) \\ge 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"170\" height=\"43\" />, so we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/a/15ae9b6f53a6daf2c9ee6a49bb76a6157102f722.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) = f\\left( \\frac{c_n -x}{f(x)} \\right) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"186\" height=\"43\" />.  This constant function clearly satisfies the given equation.  Q.E.D.\n</p><p><br />\n<i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2005_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A2&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_IMO_Shortlist_Problems\" title=\"2005 IMO Shortlist Problems\">2005 IMO Shortlist Problems</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=494821#494821\">Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018035806\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.057 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.096 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 571/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 473/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 19986/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   26.460      